Create a profile: Students select interests, skills they want to learn, and career goals.
    Has must have skills they want to learn when searching for mentor



Swipe on mentors: Ai Agent calculates compatibility using shared interests and learning goals.
    Tells students matching percentage
    Can give students mentors that dont have must haves but warns which ones
    Gives to students in order of best matching
    Can recalculate mentor compatibility based off what the student has showed interest
    

Get matched: A mentor accepts the student's request.



Complete a mini challenge: The mentor offers a 15–30-minute learning activity or feedback opportunity.



Build your portfolio: Students earn a verified contribution record showing what they completed and feedback they received.


For your hackathon, I'd focus on five screens: student profile, student peer discovery,  mentor discovery, successful match, and completed challenge with feedback.

Avoid building a full messaging system, complicated authentication, or too many separate features until that flow works.

You can use Java + Spring Boot for the matching algorithm and backend, with HTML, CSS, and JavaScript for the swipe interface.

The PDF specifically says:
"One complete flow beats five half-built features."

That's the best principle to follow when deciding what to implement.

My strongest suggestion: Make the swipe interface the exciting hook, but make the micro-challenge and earned feedback the feature that actually solves Honda's problem.
